=== Grothendieck Groups and Projective Class Groups <ss:grothendieck-projective>

Here we shall derive some basic properties of the Grothendieck group
$G_0 lr((A))$ and the projective class group $K_0 lr((A))$ of an arbitrary ring
$A$. We shall also introduce the Whitehead group $K_1 lr((A))$, to be studied in
more detail in §@sec:whitehead-groups. While we are mainly interested in the
case where $A$ is an integral group ring $R G$ of a finite group $G$ over some
domain $R$, as in §@ss:grothendieck-frobenius, it is nevertheless necessary to
treat some of the topics in greater generality.

As in §@ss:grothendieck-frobenius (see Definition
@i:def:ring-grothendieck-projective-groups), the Grothendieck group
$G_0 lr((A))$ is the additive group generated by expressions $[M]$, one for each
isomorphism class $(M)$ of f.g. left $A$-modules $M$, with relations
$[M] = [M'] + [M'']$ for each ses (short exact sequence)
$0 -> M' -> M -> M'' -> 0$ of f.g. left $A$-modules. Our first result gives a
simple criterion for deciding when two $A$-modules yield the same element in
$G_0 lr((A))$.
#nidx(
  "$G_0 lr((A)), G_0^R lr((Lambda)), G_0^t lr((Lambda))$",
  "Grothendieck groups",
)

#proposition[
  Let $A$ be an arbitrary ring, and let $M, N$ be f.g. left $A$-modules. Then
  $[M] = [N]$ in $G_0 lr((A))$ if and only if there exist a pair of ses’s (of
  f.g. left $A$-modules) of the form
  $
    0 -> X_1 -> X_2 plus.o M -> X_3 -> 0,
    quad 0 -> X_1 -> X_2 plus.o N -> X_3 -> 0.
  $
] <prop:grothendieck-equal-classes-criterion>
#proof[
  Clearly $[M] = [N]$ in $G_0 lr((A))$ whenever two such ses’s exist. Suppose
  conversely that $[M] = [N]$ in $G_0 lr((A))$, and write
  $G_0 lr((A)) = upright(bold(F)) slash upright(bold(F))_0$ as in
  @i:def:grothendieck-category-group. Then $(M) - (N) in upright(bold(F))_0$, so
  we may write
  $
    (M) - (N) = sum_i {(X_i) - (X'_i) - (X''_i)}
    - sum_j {(Y_j) - (Y'_j) - (Y''_j)},
  $
  where either summation may be empty, and where
  $
    0 -> X'_i -> X_i -> X''_i -> 0, quad
    0 -> Y'_j -> Y_j -> Y''_j -> 0
  $
  are ses’s of f.g. $A$-modules. Put $X = product.co_i X_i$,
  $X' = product.co_i X'_i$, etc., so there are ses’s
  $ 0 -> X' -> X -> X'' -> 0, quad 0 -> Y' -> Y -> Y'' -> 0 $
  <eq:grothendieck-class-witness-sequences>
  of f.g. $A$-modules. Since
  $
    (M) + sum_i {(X'_i) + (X''_i)} + sum_j (Y_j)
    = (N) + sum_i (X_i) + sum_j {(Y'_j) + (Y''_j)}
  $
  in $upright(bold(F))$, it follows that
  $
    M plus.o X' plus.o X'' plus.o Y
    tilde.equiv N plus.o X plus.o Y' plus.o Y''.
  $

  #source(31)
  Let us denote the direct sum on the right-hand side above by $S$. Since
  $M plus.o X' plus.o X'' plus.o Y tilde.equiv S$, it follows from
  @eq:grothendieck-class-witness-sequences that there exists a ses
  $ 0 -> X' plus.o Y' -> S -> M plus.o X'' plus.o Y'' -> 0. $
  There is thus a ses
  $
    0 -> X' plus.o Y' -> S plus.o N
    -> M plus.o X'' plus.o Y'' plus.o N -> 0.
  $
  Likewise, there is a ses of the same form, with middle term $S plus.o M$. This
  gives the desired result.
]

The same type of argument applies to the projective class group#idx(
  "Projective class group",
) $K_0 lr((A))$, which is the Grothendieck group of the category $cal(P)(A)$ of
f.g. projective left $A$-modules (see
@i:def:ring-grothendieck-projective-groups). Recall that $K_0 lr((A))$ is
generated by expressions $[P]$, one for each isomorphism class $(P)$ of f.g.
projective left $A$-modules $P$, with relations $[P plus.o P'] = [P] + [P']$ for
all $P, P' in cal(P)(A)$. In the analogous version of the proof of
@prop:grothendieck-equal-classes-criterion, all the ses’s which occur are split
sequences, so we obtain:
#nidx(
  "$K_0 lr((A))$",
  "projective class group",
)

#proposition[
  Let $M, N in cal(P)(A)$. Then $[M] = [N]$ in $K_0 lr((A))$ if and only if
  $ M plus.o X tilde.equiv N plus.o X quad "for some" X in cal(P)(A). $
  <eq:projective-class-stable-witness>
] <prop:projective-equal-classes-criterion>

If @eq:projective-class-stable-witness holds, then choosing $Y in cal(P)(A)$
such that $X plus.o Y$ is a free $A$-module $A^((k))$, we obtain
$ M plus.o A^((k)) tilde.equiv N plus.o A^((k)) quad "for some" k. $
We call $M$ and $N$ _stably isomorphic_#idx("Stable isomorphism") when this
occurs. We have thus shown that for $M, N in cal(P)(A)$, $[M] = [N]$ in
$K_0 lr((A))$ if and only if $M$ is stably isomorphic to $N$.

In many cases, stable isomorphism implies isomorphism. For example, if the K-S-A
Theorem @i:th:krull-schmidt-azumaya is valid for f.g. projective $A$-modules,
then from $M plus.o A^((k)) tilde.equiv N plus.o A^((k))$ we may conclude that
$M tilde.equiv N$. In particular, if $A$ is a left artinian ring, or if $A$ is
an algebra, f.g. as module over a complete d.v.r., then
@i:th:krull-schmidt-azumaya may be applied, and we conclude that stable
isomorphism is identical with ordinary isomorphism in these cases. We shall have
much more to say about this “cancellation” problem later on (see
@th:bass-cancellation).

We shall now introduce two new groups $K_1 lr((A))$ and $G_1 lr((A))$,
associated with an arbitrary ring $A$. As we shall see later, the group
$K_1 lr((A))$ is closely related to the group of units $A^dot$ of $A$. It has
important applications to topology when $A$ is an integral group ring. On the
other hand, the group $G_1 lr((A))$ is often easier to compute, and there is a
Cartan homomorphism $K_1 lr((A)) -> G_1 lr((A))$, analogous to the Cartan map
$K_0 lr((A)) -> G_0 lr((A))$, which was studied extensively in
§@i:sec:cartan-brauer-triangle.

To begin with, let $cal(C)$ be some category of left $A$-modules; the two cases
of interest are those where $cal(C) = cal(P)(A)$ and
$cal(C) = lmodule(A, op("mod"))$, the category of all f.g. left $A$-modules. We
intend to define $K_1 lr((cal(C)))$ by means of generators and relations, just
as we did in defining the Grothendieck group $K_0 lr((cal(C)))$.
#source(32)
For each $M in cal(C)$, let $Aut M$ denote the group of all $A$-automorphisms of
$M$. Consider the set of ordered pairs $(M, mu)$ with $M in cal(C)$ and
$mu in Aut M$. These pairs will be the generators of $K_1 lr((cal(C)))$, and
will be required to satisfy two types of relations. In case $A$ is a field, and
$cal(C)$ is the category of f.d. vector spaces over $A$, the relations become
the familiar axiomatic description of the determinant map for automorphisms of a
vector space. For this reason, for an arbitrary category $cal(C)$, we often call
$K_1 lr((cal(C)))$ the _determinantal $K_1$_, and write it as
$K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$.#idx("Determinantal K₁")

Consider now the collection of all ordered pairs $(M, mu)$, with $M in cal(C)$
and $mu in Aut M$. A _morphism_ $f: (M, mu) -> (N, nu)$ is, by definition, an
element $f in Hom_A lr((M, N))$ for which the diagram
#automorphism-morphism
commutes. This collection of pairs, with morphisms defined as above, constitutes
a category of modules.#footnote[
  Let $A' = A[x]$ be a polynomial ring over $A$, where $x$ is an indeterminate
  commuting with all elements of $A$. Then $(M, mu)$ may be viewed as a left
  $A'$-module $M$, on which $x$ acts as $mu$. A morphism $f: (M, mu) -> (N, nu)$
  is then just an $A'$-homomorphism $f: M -> N$. This interpretation will be
  used in our proof of @th:regular-grothendieck-k1 below.
]
#idx("Morphism", "of pairs")
A ses in this category
$ 0 -> (L, lambda) arrow.r^f (M, mu) arrow.r^g (N, nu) -> 0 $
<eq:automorphism-short-exact-sequence>
is a sequence in which $f$ and $g$ are morphisms, such that the sequence of
$A$-modules
$ 0 -> L arrow.r^f M arrow.r^g N -> 0 $
is exact. If we think of $f$ as an embedding of $L$ into $M$, and $g$ as a
natural surjection, the exactness of @eq:automorphism-short-exact-sequence means
that the automorphism $mu$ of $M$ induces an automorphism $lambda$ on the
submodule $L$, and also induces an automorphism $nu$ on the factor module
$N = M slash L$.

Now write $(M, mu) tilde.equiv (N, nu)$ if there exists a morphism
$f: (M, mu) -> (N, nu)$ such that $f: M tilde.equiv N$. Let $upright(bold(G))$
be the free abelian group generated by all isomorphism classes of ordered pairs
$(M, mu)$. Let $upright(bold(G))_0$ be the subgroup of $upright(bold(G))$
generated by all expressions
$ (M, mu) - (L, lambda) - (N, nu), $ <eq:determinantal-exact-relation>
one for each ses @eq:automorphism-short-exact-sequence, together with all
expressions
$
  (M, mu mu') - (M, mu) - (M, mu')
  quad "for all" M in cal(C), quad mu, mu' in Aut M.
$
<eq:determinantal-composition-relation>

#source(33)
Let $[M, mu]$ denote the image of $(M, mu)$ in the quotient group
$upright(bold(G)) slash upright(bold(G))_0$. We set
$ K_"det" lr((cal(C))) = upright(bold(G)) slash upright(bold(G))_0, $
<eq:determinantal-k-group>
and call $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$ the _determinantal $K$-group_ of the category
$cal(C)$. We note that $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$ is a homomorphic image of the
Grothendieck group of the category of pairs $(M, mu)$ defined above. Further,
since $[M, mu^(-1)] = -[M, mu]$ in $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$, it follows that every
element of $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$ is expressible as $[M, mu]$ for some
$M in cal(C)$ and some $mu in Aut M$.

#definition[
  The _Whitehead group_ $K_1 lr((A))$ is the additive group
  $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$, where $cal(C)$ is the category $cal(P)(A)$. On the
  other hand, define $G_1 lr((A))$ to be $K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$, with
  $cal(C) = lmodule(A, op("mod"))$.
] <def:whitehead-determinantal-group>
#idx("Whitehead group", target: [@def:whitehead-determinantal-group])
#idx("Determinantal K₁", target: [@def:whitehead-determinantal-group])
#idx("G₁(A)", target: [@def:whitehead-determinantal-group])

(Some authors use the term “Whitehead group” to refer to the group
$op("Wh")(G) = K_1 lr((ZZ G)) slash {plus.minus G^(a b)}$, where $G$ is a finite
group. The group $op("Wh")(G)$ will be discussed in
§@sec:whitehead-integral-group-rings.)

Now let $A$ be a semisimple ring. It is clear that
$K_0 lr((A)) tilde.equiv G_0 lr((A))$, and the latter is a free abelian group on
$s$ generators, where $s$ is the number of distinct simple left $A$-modules. We
conclude this subsection with the computation of $K_1 lr((A))$. This result is
of intrinsic interest, and will be used repeatedly in later discussion. Let
$A = plus.o.big A_i$, where the ${A_i}$ are the Wedderburn components of $A$.
Each $M in cal(P)(A)$ decomposes into a direct sum $M = plus.o.big M_i$, with
$M_i in cal(P)(A_i)$, and each $mu in Aut M$ has a corresponding decomposition.
It follows at once that
$ K_1 lr((A)) tilde.equiv product.co_i K_1 lr((A_i)), $
<eq:whitehead-wedderburn-decomposition>
so the problem of evaluating $K_1$ reduces to the case of simple artinian rings.

Changing notation, let $A$ be a simple artinian ring. Then (see @i:th:morita)
$A$ is Morita equivalent to a division ring $D$, and we have
$ K_1 lr((D)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A)). $ <eq:whitehead-morita-isomorphism>
Explicitly, write $A = End_D V$ where $V$ is a f.d. right vector space over $D$.
There is a bijection between the isomorphism classes in $cal(P)(D)$ and
$cal(P)(A)$, which is given by mapping $M in cal(P)(D)$ onto
$V times.o_D M in cal(P)(A)$. This bijection yields an isomorphism
$ Aut_D M tilde.equiv Aut_A lr((V times.o_D M)). $
The isomorphism in @eq:whitehead-morita-isomorphism is then given by
$
  [M, mu] -> [V times.o_D M, 1 times.o mu]
  quad "for" M in cal(P)(D), quad mu in Aut_D M.
$

In order to calculate $K_1 lr((D))$, where $D$ is a skewfield, we make use of
Dieudonné determinants (see the discussion in
§@i:sec:separable-algebras-splitting-fields following
@i:eq:reduced-norm-dieudonne-determinant). Let $D^dot = D - {0}$, and set
$ D^hash = D^dot slash [D^dot, D^dot], $ <eq:skewfield-unit-abelianization>
an abelian multiplicative group.
#source(34)

#theorem[
  The Dieudonné determinant gives an isomorphism of groups
  $ K_1 lr((D)) tilde.equiv D^hash $
  for every skewfield $D$.
] <th:whitehead-skewfield-determinant>
#proof[
  Consider an ordered pair $(M, mu)$, where $M in cal(P)(D)$ and
  $mu in Aut_D M$. Each $M in cal(P)(D)$ has a finite $D$-basis, relative to
  which $mu$ may be represented by a matrix $bold(X) in GL_n lr((D))$, where
  $n = dim_D M$. Change of basis replaces $bold(X)$ by
  $bold(T) bold(X) bold(T)^(-1)$ for some $bold(T) in GL_n lr((D))$. If “det”
  denotes the Dieudonné determinant of a matrix, then $det bold(X) in D^hash$,
  and
  $ det bold(X) = det bold(T) bold(X) bold(T)^(-1). $
  Setting $det mu = det bold(X) in D^hash$, the Dieudonné determinant $det mu$
  is thus well defined.

  Now let @eq:automorphism-short-exact-sequence be a ses, where $L, M, N$ are
  $D$-spaces; we may choose a $D$-basis of $M$ so that the matrix $bold(X)$
  representing $mu$ has the form
  $ bold(X) = mat(bold(X)_1, ast; bold(0), bold(X)_2), $
  where $bold(X)_1$ is the matrix of $lambda$, and $bold(X)_2$ that of $nu$.
  Then $det bold(X) = (det bold(X)_1)(det bold(X)_2)$, that is,
  $ det mu = (det lambda)(det nu). $
  Likewise,
  $ det mu mu' = (det mu)(det mu') quad "for" mu, mu' in Aut M. $
  It follows that there is a well-defined homomorphism
  $ det: K_1 lr((D)) -> D^hash, $
  which maps $[M, mu]$ onto $det mu$. The map is obviously surjective. To show
  it is injective, suppose that $det mu = 1$, and let $mu$ be represented by
  some matrix $bold(X)$. Then we can find products $bold(P)$ and $bold(Q)$ of
  elementary matrices over $D$, such that
  $ bold(P) bold(X) bold(Q) = op("diag")(1, dots, 1, d) $
  for some $d in [D^dot, D^dot]$. But every elementary matrix $bold(E)$
  represents the zero element of $K_1 lr((D))$, since $bold(E)$ is of the form
  $
    mat(bold(I), ast; bold(0), bold(I)) quad "or" quad
    mat(bold(I), bold(0); ast, bold(I)),
  $
  and hence corresponds to some ses
  $ 0 -> (X_1, 1) -> (X_2, ast) -> (X_3, 1) -> 0, $
  with $[X_1, 1] = [X_3, 1] = 0$ in $K_1 lr((D))$. Finally,
  $op("diag")(1, dots, 1, d)$ represents the zero element in $K_1 lr((D))$,
  since $d in [D^dot, D^dot]$ and $K_1 lr((D))$ is abelian. Thus, if
  $det mu = 1$, then $[M, mu] = 0$ in $K_1 lr((D))$. This completes the proof
  that $det: K_1 lr((D)) tilde.equiv D^hash$.
]

#source(35)
#remarks(numbered: false)[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The above argument also shows that every element of $K_1 lr((D))$ has the
      form $[D, d_r]$ for some $d in D^dot$, where $d_r$ is right multiplication
      by $d$.],
    [Klingenberg @bib:Klingenberg1962 has extended this theory to the case of a
      local ring $R$, not necessarily commutative. Set
      $R^hash = R^dot slash [R^dot, R^dot]$, where $R^dot$ is the group of units
      of $R$. There is a well-defined isomorphism
      $det: K_1 lr((R)) tilde.equiv R^hash$, similar to that above.],
  )
] <rem:whitehead-local-ring-determinant>
